  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 419 AM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over Northwestern Ontario,\nwith high over the Northern Rockies nudging into the Northern\nPlains. Upper level analysis places low over northwest Ontario,\nwith ridge over the northwestern CONUS. Notable short wave to\nthe southwest of the low now passing over Manitoba into the\nDakotas continues to produce some showers and a few\nthunderstorms over central and eastern North Dakota which are\nmoving quickly off to the east.\n\nFor today, a cooler airmass settling in behind yesterday's\nsystem will bring near to a bit below average temperatures to\nthe area, while upper ridge to the west should keep conditions \ndry. \n\nOn Thursday, upper trough digging into British Columbia pushes\nsoutheastward, with upper ridge pushing over into our area.\nTemperatures will be notably warmer, with highs in the 80s over\nmost locations, and dry conditions continuing. \n\nA cold front pushes through late Thursday night into early\nFriday, resulting in increasing northwesterly winds in its wake.\nWith the easterly pushing upper trough, a few showers may\ndevelop, primarily over northern locations. \n\nNotably cooler temperatures are then expected this weekend into\nearly next week. Quiet weather is expected through the day on\nSaturday, before a Pacific low quickly moves onshore and pushes\ntowards the Rockies, while a surface low develops to the lee of\nthe Rockies and gradually meanders easterly. Exact placement of\nsome of these features remains uncertain, but this general \npattern does result in elevated precipitation chances over our \narea for the second half of the weekend into the start of the \nupcoming work week.",
